mgrep: search files for lines that match a pattern

usage: mgrep [OPTIONS] PATTERN [PATH...]
       mgrep [OPTIONS] -e PATTERN [PATH...]
       mgrep [OPTIONS] -f FILE [PATH...]

Directories are searched recursively, each one's entries in byte order, and
files that hold a NUL byte are skipped. With no PATH, mgrep searches the
current directory. The output is the same whatever the number of carriers.

Options:
  --ignore-case           Match case-insensitively (-i)
  --fixed-strings         Take the patterns as fixed strings, not regular expressions (-F)
  --word-regexp           Match whole words only (-w)
  --invert-match          Print the lines that do not match (-v)
  --count                 Print the number of matching lines in each file (-c)
  --count-matches         Print the number of matches in each file
  --files-with-matches    Print only the names of the files that match (-l)
  --only-matching         Print each match on a line of its own (-o)
  --line-number           Print line numbers, which is the default (-n)
  --no-line-number        Leave out line numbers (-N)
  --column                Print the terminal column where each line's first match starts
  --heading               Print each file's name once, above its lines
  --with-filename         Print the file name on every line, even for one file (-H)
  --no-filename           Never print file names
  --after-context <int>   Print this many lines after each match (-A)
  --before-context <int>  Print this many lines before each match (-B)
  --context <int>         Print this many lines before and after each match (-C)
  --max-count <int>       Stop reading a file after this many matching lines (-m)
  --quiet                 Print nothing, and stop at the first match (-q)
  --threads <int>         Search with this many tasks; 4 when not given (-j)
  --max-open <int>        Keep at most this many files open at once; 8 when not given
  --regexp <str>          The pattern, when it starts with a dash (-e)
  --file <str>            Read the patterns from this file, one a line; - is standard input (-f)
  --replace <str>         Print each matching line with its matches replaced; $1 and $name expand groups (-r)
  --group <int>           With --only-matching, print this capture group of each match
  --color <str>           Colour output: always or never; never when not given
  --hidden                Search names that start with a dot (-.)
  --glob <str>            Search only files whose names match one of these comma-separated globs (-g)
  --max-depth <int>       Descend at most this many directory levels below each path (-d)
  --max-filesize <int>    Skip files larger than this many bytes
  --binary                Search files that hold NUL bytes as well (-a)
  --path-separator <str>  Print paths with this separator instead of the system's
  --stats                 Print a table of the matching files and the totals at the end
  --help                  Print this help (-h)
  --version               Print the version (-V)
Arguments:
  <args>...               PATTERN, then the files and directories to search; - is standard input

Short forms: -i -F -w -v -c -l -o -n -N -H -q -a -. -h -V, and -A N -B N -C N
-m N -j N -e PAT -f FILE -r TEXT -g GLOBS -d N, which group as in -iw or -C2.
--name=value is --name value.

Exit status: 0 when a line matched, 1 when none did, 2 on an error.
